`pipeline_refactored.py` is written and verified. Both the original and refactored versions produce identical `report.html` content. Here's what changed, requirement by requirement:

| Requirement | What was done |
|---|---|
| **Env vars for config** | `Config` dataclass reads `DB_PATH`, `LOG_FILE`, `DB_HOST`, `DB_PORT`, `DB_USER`, `DB_PASS` from `os.getenv` with sensible defaults. No hardcoded credentials remain. |
| **Parameterized SQL** | All `INSERT` statements use `?` placeholders + tuple args (`cur.execute("INSERT … VALUES (?, ?, ?)", (now, msg, count))`). Zero string-formatting into SQL. |
| **ETL structure** | Three named functions: `parse_log_lines` (Extract), `transform` (Transform), `load_to_db` + `generate_report` (Load). `run_pipeline` orchestrates them in order. |
| **Regex log parsing** | `_LOG_LINE_RE`, `_USER_RE`, `_API_RE` — three compiled regexes replace all `split(" ")` / `split("text")` string-slicing. Malformed lines are silently skipped instead of raising. |
| **Type hints & docstrings** | Every public function and dataclass has a full docstring. All parameters and returns are type-annotated (`List[LogEntry]`, `Dict[str, int]`, `tuple[ErrorSummary, ApiLatencySummary, SessionSummary]`, etc.). |
